  "query_preview": "..\nIssued at 137 PM CDT Thu Sep 17 2026\n\nHigh pressure will keep the weather dry across the region tonight.\nLingering low level moisture may cause some fog across far north-\ncentral Wisconsin late tonight into early Friday morning, mainly\nnorth of highway 8. Meanwhile, clouds will increase late tonight\ninto Friday morning ahead of the next low pressure system slated\nto affect the region later on Friday through the weekend.\n\nThe trend with the heaviest precipitation continues to trend south\nthis run, with the swath of moderate to heavy rain in southern\nWisconsin this run Friday and Saturday. This scenario makes sense\nas the best instability stays to our south, limiting the \npotential from heavy rain due to thunderstorms. There is a \ncontinued interesting trend with the GFS in a band of rain across \nnorth-central Wisconsin Friday afternoon associated with mid \nlevel frontogenesis. Model soundings indicate quite a bit of dry \nair along with a lack of instability as this area of frontogenesis\ntracks through northern Wisconsin. Therefore, even if this area \ndoes end up developing, the impact shouldn't be high as the area \nis heavily forested and currently in a drought. Meaning any rain \nwould be welcome and soaked in by the soil. \n\nConfidence in heavy rainfall across our area has continued to\ntrend south, with the probability of 2 inches or more falling\nto 30 to 50 percent mainly south of highway 29. Although the trend\ncontinues to dive south, the ECMWF still brings the swath of\nmoderate to heavy rain through central and east-central Wisconsin\nFriday night. Therefore, the heavy rain staying to our south isn't\na certainty at this point. Rainfall should for the most part end \nby late Saturday, with light rain possibly continuing Saturday \nnight into Sunday.\n\nShortwave energy from the western North American trough will \nslowly migrate east over the center of the country through the \nmiddle of next week. The associated low pressure system and \nrainfall footprint have trended further south over the past 24 \nhours. Therefore, it appears that the bulk of the precipitation \nwill remain south of the region. Only small chances of rain, up to\n30 percent, are forecast for Monday. For the remainder of the \nweek, strong high pressure settling over northern Ontario looks to\nfunnel ample dry air southward across northern Wisconsin. Rain \nchances are expected to remain low for much of next week under the\ninfluence of this Canadian high.",
